    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d930">

                <pb facs="0976" n="858" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg858"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.7331A15/25: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Venezuela</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-10-26T12:00:00-04:30">October 26,
                        1929—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 10:35 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>172. Legation’s 162, October 3, 8 p.m.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d930-f1"/> All
                    America Cables has now received permit in writing to operate French cable to
                    Venezuela and to lay new cable from La Guayra to Curaçao and thence to
                    Maracaibo. Duration of nineteen to twenty years and the tax payable to the
                    Venezuelan Government has been fixed at twelve and a half percent on the gross
                    receipts exclusive of government messages. Company agrees not to charge less
                    than government radio rate.</p>
                <p>This brings to a successful conclusion negotiations since 1922 and gives
                    Venezuela a modern cable service which for the first time links it up with All
                    America System.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
